---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WebpToJpg from '@/components/tools/image-convert/WebpToJpg.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="webp-to-jpg">
  <WebpToJpg client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert WebP to JPG</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your .webp files onto the box above. Batches of any size work.</li>
      <li>Choose a background colour. WebP can be transparent and JPEG cannot, so transparent pixels are blended onto the colour you pick (white by default).</li>
      <li>Set the quality. The default of 85 is higher than usual because the source has already been through one lossy codec.</li>
      <li>Save each JPG, or download all of them as a single ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why convert WebP to JPG</h2>
    <p>
      JPEG is the most widely readable image format. Every operating system, camera, printer, photo kiosk,
      email client and web form since the mid-nineties accepts it. WebP has full browser support, but many
      desktop applications and upload forms still refuse it. If a program or form rejects your file, convert it
      to JPG.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost is size. The same photo as a JPEG is normally 25–35% larger than the WebP, because WebP's coder
      is more efficient.
    </p>

    <h2>Transparency: what your background choice does</h2>
    <p>
      A baseline JPEG stores only three colour channels, so it cannot mark a pixel as see-through. The tool
      blends each transparent or semi-transparent pixel in the WebP with your chosen colour by its alpha value,
      so a shadow that fades from 60% to 0% opacity fades smoothly into the background colour.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th align="left">Where the image will be used</th>
            <th align="left">Suggested background</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Word document, printed page, most web pages</td><td>White (#ffffff)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dark-themed slide deck or app screenshot</td><td>Near-black (#1e293b or #000000)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Product listing on a marketplace</td><td>White; most platforms require it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Overlaying a coloured panel</td><td>Sample that exact colour and paste the hex value</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If the image will appear on more than one background colour, do not use JPEG. Use
      <a href="/webp-to-png">WebP to PNG</a> and keep the alpha channel.
    </p>

    <h2>Quality guidance for a second-generation encode</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>85–90: the recommended range. The second lossy pass adds no visible artefacts to the first.</li>
      <li>80: fine for casual sharing and messaging, where the image is re-compressed anyway.</li>
      <li>Below 70: the two generations of artefacts add up, most visibly as blocks in flat areas and halos around text.</li>
      <li>95 and above: use only if the JPG is an intermediate step before more editing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For lossless output, use <a href="/webp-to-png">WebP to PNG</a>. To convert a folder of WebP, AVIF and PNG
      files in one pass, use the <a href="/image-converter">universal image converter</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
